---
import Work from "@layouts/Work.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
    order: 4,
    title: "Go Quest",
    coverImage: "/goquest/Go Quest.jpg",
};
---

<Work
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="A Playable Immersive Environment Exploring Spatial Logic and Sensory Interaction"
    description=""
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Architectural Studio VII"
    courseDescription="Inception"
    advisor="Carla Leitao"
    collaborators="Odette Gonzalez & Sarah Lowrey"
    date="Fall 2023"
    location="Troy, New York"
    gallery={import.meta.glob("/public/goquest/gallery/*")}
>
    <section class="stack">
        <p>
            Go Quest originated as an exploration of immersive space—how
            environments can shape perception, emotion, and behavior. Early
            studies examined dreams, sensory distortion, and the psychology of
            fear, using AI-generated imagery to visualize dream fragments and
            test how abstraction, atmosphere, and ambiguity influence emotional
            response. These experiments led to investigations into discomfort
            and restlessness, drawing inspiration from unsettling media such as
            the “I'm Poppy” video, whose repetitive calmness and uncanny tone
            produced visible unease among viewers. Additional research examined
            immersive simulations of mental disorders, including games designed
            to approximate the perceptual distortions experienced by individuals
            with schizophrenia. The team also studied the anechoic “room of
            silence,” a space so acoustically absorbent that the absence of
            stimuli causes the brain to generate its own—an effect that induces
            anxiety in some and calm in others.
        </p>
        <p>
            These independent explorations formed the conceptual foundation for
            a project centered on immersion as a spatial condition, not merely a
            visual effect. When the team transitioned into collaborative
            development, the question became: How can immersion be constructed,
            controlled, and manipulated through interaction? With access to the
            Craive Lab—a research facility equipped with high-end 360°
            projection technology—the project naturally shifted toward digital
            methods. Unity became the medium through which spatial ideas could
            be tested, refined, and ultimately experienced as a fully
            interactive environment.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Precedents and Influences</h3>
        <p>
            Three key references shaped the conceptual and experiential
            framework of Go Quest:
        </p>
        <div class="grid sidebar pattern">
            <iframe
                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/o9LNCRZYvNQ"
                title="YouTube video player"
                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
                referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
                allowfullscreen></iframe>
            <h4>Inviting Chaos</h4>
            <p>
                TeamLab's Borderless introduced the idea of environments that
                behave independently of the user. Through projections, mirrors,
                and LED-driven infinity effects, the installation creates a
                world where elements move, react, and evolve without direct
                player control. This sense of boundlessness and unpredictability
                informed Go Quest's approach to environmental autonomy.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid sidebar pattern">
            <iframe
                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/t4TTuRs0BWA"
                title="YouTube video player"
                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
                referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
                allowfullscreen></iframe>
            <h4>Trial and Error</h4>
            <p>
                Dark Echo inspired the use of darkness, sound, and discovery
                through action. In a world without visual cues, players navigate
                by producing sound that reveals spatial boundaries. This
                mechanic—akin to echolocation—reinforced the idea that
                exploration should be learned, not instructed. Go Quest adopts
                this philosophy by allowing players to uncover the world piece
                by piece.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid sidebar pattern">
            <iframe
                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/QH05S88nhG0"
                title="YouTube video player"
                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
                referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
                allowfullscreen></iframe>
            <h4>Contained Tools</h4>
            <p>
                Monument Valley demonstrated the power of compact, legible
                stages where players experiment with tools to progress. Its
                clarity and controlled puzzle logic offered a counterpoint to
                Borderless's chaos. Go Quest positions itself between these
                extremes, blending unpredictability with moments of agency.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>From Research to Mechanics</h3>
        <p>
            The final game world is built from a field of modular box tiles that
            function as both spatial units and event triggers. Each tile type
            embodies a specific behavior, creating a system where interaction
            generates complexity:
        </p>
        <div class="grid auto-fit">
            <div class="stack">
                <img src="/goquest/Basic Tile.png" alt="Basic Tile" />
                <h4>Basic Tile</h4>
                <p>
                    The core spatial unit; its glowing edges orient the player
                    in darkness.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
                <img src="/goquest/Light Tile.png" alt="Light Tile" />
                <h4>Light Tile</h4>
                <p>
                    Illuminates on contact, mapping the player's path and
                    forming a spatial memory.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
                <img src="/goquest/Trap Tile.png" alt="Trap Tile" />
                <h4>Trap Tile</h4>
                <p>
                    Vanishes when stepped on, opening a void that may cause the
                    player to fall and respawn.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
                <img src="/goquest/Colored Tiles.png" alt="Colored Tile" />
                <h4>Colored Tiles</h4>
                <p>
                    Encode progress through shifting hues, creating a visual
                    hierarchy tied to movement and time.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
                <img src="/goquest/Reactive Tiles.png" alt="Reactive Tile" />
                <h4>Reactive Tiles</h4>
                <p>
                    Activate, resize, or extrude other tiles, revealing new
                    platforms and expanding the navigable world.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p>
            This taxonomy functions like an architectural material palette—each
            element has a behavior, a purpose, and a role in shaping the
            environment.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Immersion and Sensory Design</h3>
        <div class="row">
            <p>
                Sound plays a central role in constructing immersion.
                Atmospheric audio establishes tone, while reactive sound effects
                respond to player movement, reinforcing the connection between
                user and environment. Pulsating rhythms mark the passage of
                time, and subtle hums from glowing tiles create a layered
                soundscape that deepens presence. The interplay of darkness,
                illumination, and sound transforms navigation into a sensory
                experience rather than a purely visual one.
            </p>
            <img src="/goquest/Sound Effects.png" alt="Sound Effects" />
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Gameplay and Spatial Evolution</h3>
        <p>
            Players begin in darkness, illuminated only by a grid of tiles. Each
            step lights the tile beneath them, creating a glowing trace that
            records their movement. Tile functions activate on contact, layering
            interaction over exploration. Reaching the periphery triggers the
            first major event: the revelation of spatial bounds beyond the
            ground plane.
        </p>
        <p>
            Players begin in darkness, illuminated only by a grid of tiles. Each
            step lights the tile beneath them, creating a glowing trace that
            records their movement. Tile functions activate on contact, layering
            interaction over exploration. Reaching the periphery triggers the
            first major event: the revelation of spatial bounds beyond the
            ground plane.
        </p>
        <p>
            By the time all triggers activate, the relationship between player
            and world has fundamentally changed. What began as a predictable
            environment becomes a complex, shifting system that challenges
            perception, memory, and control.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Immersive Presentation</h3>
        <p>
            The final version of Go Quest was presented in the Craive Lab's 360°
            projection environment, allowing the game to be experienced as a
            spatial installation. The surrounding projections amplified the
            game's atmospheric qualities, immersing players in a world where
            sound, light, and movement enveloped them from all sides. This
            setting reinforced the project's origins in immersive research and
            demonstrated how digital environments can operate as architectural
            experiences.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Conclusion</h3>
        <p>
            What began as an abstract study of dreams, emotions, and sensory
            immersion evolved into a fully interactive Unity-built world. Go
            Quest merges architectural thinking with game design, using spatial
            logic, behavioral systems, and sensory cues to construct an
            environment that unfolds through exploration. It stands as both a
            game and a spatial experiment—an investigation into how immersion
            can be designed, manipulated, and experienced.
        </p>
    </section>
</Work>
